/* ============================================================================
   linear-regression.epher -- fit a line to study hours versus exam score

   Fits the least-squares line y = b1 * x + b0 to the seven (hours, score)
   pairs in `xs` and `ys` with the builtin linreg (guide 1.20), which
   prints the fitted model and the correlation r as a display string. The
   slope says a study hour is worth about 5.7 score points over this
   range, and r = 0.9981 marks a very tight fit. The data are example
   values, not measurements.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/regression/linear-regression.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\regression\linear-regression.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/regression/linear-regression.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The knobs to edit are `xs` and `ys` below. Everything above the results
   section is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const xs = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7}     // hours of study, one per student
const ys = {55, 63, 68, 74, 79, 85, 90}   // exam scores out of 100, same order

// ---- the fitted model -------------------------------------------------------------
linreg(xs, ys)      // least-squares line, printed with its correlation r

/* ---- expected output ----
= {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7}
= {55, 63, 68, 74, 79, 85, 90}
= y = 5.7143*x + 50.5714 (r = 0.9981)
*/